{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.PairingSeesCommutator","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.PairingSeesCommutator","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Notation","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Rat.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FinCases"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.eq_of_pairing_eq_of_nondegenerate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.eq_of_pairing_eq_of_nondegenerate","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PairingSeesCommutator.lean#L94-L112","doc":"**Uniqueness of a Riesz representative.**\n\nIf the pairing `B` is nondegenerate in the second slot, two elements that pair\nidentically against everything are equal.\n\nThis is the epistemically load-bearing statement. The probe solves a linear\nsystem for a representing element `y₀` and finds it; on its own that is a\n*computation*, and a computation that produced one answer among many would be a\nfitted choice. Nondegeneracy is what upgrades it: there is at most one such\nelement, so `y₀` is determined by `net` and the pairing together, with nothing\nleft to select. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.diag_one_neg_one_is_commutator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.diag_one_neg_one_is_commutator","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PairingSeesCommutator.lean#L83-L90","doc":"`diag(1,-1)` really is a commutator, so the guard above is about the\ncommutator subspace and not merely about some traceless matrix. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.trace_pairing_sees_a_commutator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.trace_pairing_sees_a_commutator","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PairingSeesCommutator.lean#L62-L80","doc":"**The converse guard.** The trace *pairing* `⟨x,y⟩ = tr(x*y)` does not\nvanish on the commutator subspace, even though the trace itself does.\n\nExhibited on `d = diag(1,-1) ∈ sl₂(ℚ)`, which is a genuine commutator\n(`d = e*f - f*e` for the standard nilpotents, proved below):\n\n* `trace d = 0` — invisible to the linear functional;\n* `trace (d * d) = 2 ≠ 0` — visible to the bilinear one.\n\nWithout this theorem `cyclic_annihilates_commutator` reads as an impossibility\nresult. With it, the two together say the remainder needs a pairing rather than\na functional — which is the actual content. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.cyclic_annihilates_commutator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.cyclic_annihilates_commutator","kind":"theorem","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PairingSeesCommutator.lean#L48-L57","doc":"A **cyclic** functional annihilates every commutator.\n\nThis is the obstruction in one line: `τ` need not be a Markov trace, an\naugmentation or a character — cyclicity alone is enough to make the commutator\nsubspace invisible. So the failure is not a defect of a particular trace, and no\nsearch over traces can repair it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}